    },
    {
      "step_number": 2,
      "step_title": "Discomfort Causal Mechanism Elaboration",
      "step_description": "Explain that a state of cognitive dissonance arises when the two cognitive elements are psychologically incompatible, such that the negation of one would logically follow from the other. This violation of the preferred state of cognitive coherence triggers aversive, unpleasant physiological and psychological arousal (the discomfort in question). Per Elliot Aronson’s later refinement of the theory, this discomfort is amplified when the contradiction threatens the individual’s positive self-concept as a consistent, moral, and competent person."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "step_title": "Discomfort Intensity Moderator Analysis",
      "step_description": "Specify theory-derived factors that explain variability in the severity of experienced discomfort: (1) the personal importance of the conflicting belief and action (e.g., discomfort is stronger if animal welfare is a core personal value); (2) the ratio of dissonant cognitive elements to consonant (consistency-supporting) elements (e.g., discomfort is reduced if the individual only eats meat on rare occasions); (3) perceived personal agency and responsibility for the action (e.g., discomfort is minimal if the individual was forced to eat meat due to food scarcity, as external justification reduces dissonance); (4) the perceived irreversibility of the action (e.g., permanent, high-stakes actions cause stronger discomfort)."
