  "illustrative_valid_market_failure_example": "Unregulated industrial water pollution (negative production externality): A factory’s private production costs only include labor, raw materials, and energy, and do not account for the social cost of pollution (e.g., increased local public health costs, reduced commercial fishery yields, contaminated drinking water). As a result, the factory produces far more output than is socially optimal, creating a deadweight welfare loss that the market will not correct on its own because the factory has no incentive to internalize the pollution cost without regulation or a Pigouvian tax.",
