{
  "approach_overview": "To identify the substance with the highest boiling point, we use the core relationship between intermolecular force (IMF) strength and boiling point: stronger intermolecular attractions require more thermal energy to overcome, resulting in a higher boiling point. We follow a structured 4-step process:",
  "step_1_define_IMF_strength_hierarchy": "All options are neutral covalent molecules, so we use the standard ranking of IMF strength from strongest to weakest: Hydrogen bonding (a strong dipole-dipole interaction exclusive to molecules with H covalently bonded to N, O, or F) > permanent dipole-dipole interactions (between asymmetric polar molecules) > London dispersion forces (LDF: weak induced dipoles present in all molecules, the only force in nonpolar molecules; strength increases with molar mass)",
  "step_2_classify_IMFs_for_each_candidate": {
    "A) Methane (CH₄)": "Symmetric tetrahedral nonpolar molecule with no permanent dipole and no capacity for hydrogen bonding. Only very weak London dispersion forces act between CH₄ molecules.",
    "B) Ammonia (NH₃)": "Polar trigonal pyramidal molecule with H bonded to N, so it exhibits hydrogen bonding. However, N is less electronegative than O, so N-H bond dipoles are weaker than O-H dipoles. NH₃ also only has 1 lone pair on N to act as a hydrogen bond acceptor, limiting the number of strong interactions per molecule.",
    "C) Water (H₂O)": "Polar bent molecule with H bonded to highly electronegative O, so it exhibits strong hydrogen bonding. O has 2 lone pairs to act as hydrogen bond acceptors and 2 bound H atoms to act as hydrogen bond donors, allowing each H₂O molecule to form twice as many hydrogen bonds as NH₃ on average. The higher electronegativity of O also makes each O-H hydrogen bond stronger than N-H hydrogen bonds.",
    "D) Carbon dioxide (CO₂)": "Symmetric linear nonpolar molecule (dipoles of the two C=O bonds cancel out completely) with no permanent dipole and no capacity for hydrogen bonding. Only London dispersion forces act between CO₂ molecules; while its LDF is stronger than CH₄ due to higher molar mass, it is still far weaker than hydrogen bonding."
  },
  "step_3_rank_IMF_strength_and_corresponding_boiling_points": "Ranked from strongest to weakest IMFs (and thus highest to lowest boiling point): H₂O (extensive, strong hydrogen bonding network) > NH₃ (weaker, less extensive hydrogen bonding) > CO₂ (moderate LDF) > CH₄ (very weak LDF)",
  "step_4_empirical_validation_optional": "Measured boiling points (at 1 atm) confirm the ranking: H₂O = 100°C, NH₃ = -33.3°C, CO₂ = -78.5°C (sublimes; boiling point at 5.1 atm = -56.6°C), CH₄ = -161.5°C",
  "final_conclusion": "Option C (Water, H₂O) has the highest boiling point"
}

