{
  "case_summary": "65-year-old female with type 2 diabetes and hypertension (major coronary artery disease risk factors) presenting with sudden onset chest pain and shortness of breath, with ECG showing ST-segment elevation in inferior leads (II, III, aVF).",
  "key_diagnostic_confirmation": "This clinical presentation is diagnostic of ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI), specifically inferior STEMI. A critical associated finding in ~30-40% of inferior STEMIs is concurrent right ventricular (RV) infarction, which directly impacts management safety and priorities.",
  "core_management_principles_for_acute_stemi": "Time-sensitive, mortality-lowering priorities for STEMI management, in order of immediate clinical priority, are: 1) Inhibit further platelet aggregation (the primary driver of occlusive coronary thrombus in STEMI), 2) Initiate urgent reperfusion therapy to restore coronary flow, 3) Prevent further thrombus propagation with anticoagulation, 4) Symptom and hemodynamic support (with specific caution for preload-dependent RV physiology in inferior STEMI).",
  "option_by_option_analysis": {
    "Option A (Administer nitroglycerin)": {
      "correct": false,
      "rationale": "Nitroglycerin is a venous vasodilator that reduces cardiac preload. Inferior STEMI carries high risk of concurrent RV infarction, where RV cardiac output is entirely dependent on adequate preload. Nitroglycerin-induced preload reduction can cause severe, life-threatening hypotension in this setting. Even in the absence of RV infarction, nitroglycerin is only used for symptomatic pain relief and is not a life-saving first-line priority for acute STEMI."
    },
    "Option B (Perform immediate coronary angiography)": {
      "correct": false,
      "rationale": "Coronary angiography is performed as part of primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), the preferred reperfusion strategy for STEMI. However, immediate dual antiplatelet therapy is required prior to angiography or PCI to reduce the risk of procedural thrombus formation and stent thrombosis. Arrangements for angiography occur in parallel with or immediately after administration of life-saving antiplatelet therapy, so it is not the first next step."
    },
    "Option C (Administer aspirin and clopidogrel)": {
      "correct": true,
      "rationale": "Dual antiplatelet therapy with a chewable aspirin loading dose (162-325mg) and a P2Y12 inhibitor loading dose (e.g., clopidogrel 300-600mg) is the highest priority immediate intervention for confirmed STEMI. Per ACC/AHA and ESC guidelines, dual antiplatelet therapy is administered immediately upon STEMI diagnosis: it rapidly inhibits platelet aggregation, reduces thrombus burden, and reduces all-cause mortality by ~25% in acute STEMI. This intervention is a prerequisite for safe subsequent reperfusion therapy (PCI or fibrinolysis)."
    },
    "Option D (Administer intravenous heparin)": {
      "correct": false,
      "rationale": "Intravenous heparin is an anticoagulant used to prevent further thrombus propagation in STEMI, but it is adjuvant therapy administered after dual antiplatelet therapy. Platelet inhibition is the more foundational and time-sensitive intervention for acute occlusive coronary thrombus, so heparin is not the appropriate first next step."
    }
  },
  "final_correct_answer": "C",
  "key_takeaway": "Dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) is the immediate first priority in management of confirmed STEMI, preceding all reperfusion interventions. Inferior STEMI requires specific caution with preload-reducing agents like nitroglycerin due to the high risk of concurrent right ventricular infarction."
}

