{
  "QuestionStem": "A student pushes a stationary 10 kg cardboard box across a flat, frictionless classroom floor, applying a constant horizontal force of 20 N for 1.5 seconds before releasing the box entirely. Assume air resistance is negligible. Based on Newton's laws of motion, which of the following statements is correct?",
  "Options": {
    "A": "While the student is pushing the box, the box exerts a 20 N horizontal force back on the student, directed opposite to the student's push.",
    "B": "After the student releases the box, the box will gradually slow down and come to a complete stop within 3 seconds, because moving objects naturally lose momentum without an applied force.",
    "C": "While the student is pushing the box, the box accelerates at 20 m/s², as described by Newton's second law of motion (F=ma).",
    "D": "The box only begins moving after the student's applied force exceeds the box's inertia, because inertia is an internal force that resists changes to an object's motion."
  },
  "CorrectAnswer": "A",
  "Explanation": "Option A is correct per Newton's third law of motion, which states that for every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force: the student's 20 N push on the box is matched by a 20 N force from the box on the student in the opposite direction. Option B is incorrect because Newton's first law (law of inertia) dictates that an object with no net force acting on it will maintain constant velocity; on a frictionless floor with negligible air resistance, the released box will move at a constant speed indefinitely. Option C is incorrect because rearranging Newton's second law (F=ma) gives acceleration a = F/m = 20 N / 10 kg = 2 m/s², not 20 m/s². Option D is incorrect because inertia is a property of matter (not a force) that describes an object's resistance to changes in motion; any non-zero net force will cause the stationary box to accelerate, with no need to 'exceed' inertia."
}

{
  "Question": "A college junior named Lila has strongly believed for three years that single-use plastic products cause catastrophic environmental harm, and she regularly volunteers with local anti-plastic advocacy groups. This semester, Lila accepted a paid marketing internship at a major beverage company that exclusively uses single-use plastic bottles for all its products; she took the role because it is the only major-related paid internship she was offered for the semester. When her volunteer group coordinator points out the apparent mismatch between her long-held environmental values and her new role, Lila feels significant mental discomfort and distress. Per the theory of cognitive dissonance, which of the following options correctly identifies the source of Lila’s discomfort and a typical strategy she would use to reduce that discomfort?\nA) The discomfort stems from cognitive dissonance; Lila may adjust her core belief to claim that single-use plastic is not actually as environmentally harmful as she previously thought\nB) The discomfort stems from confirmation bias; Lila may quit her internship immediately to prioritize her volunteer advocacy work\nC) The discomfort stems from operant conditioning; Lila may tell her coordinator that the beverage company plans to switch to recyclable aluminum packaging in the next 5 years\nD) The discomfort stems from self-serving bias; Lila may argue that she only took the internship because she had no other major-related work options for the semester",
  "CorrectAnswer": "A",
  "Rationale": "Option A is correct. Cognitive dissonance describes the psychological discomfort experienced when an individual holds two or more conflicting cognitions (e.g., beliefs, attitudes, behaviors), which matches Lila’s distress from the mismatch between her anti-plastic values and her new internship role. A well-documented strategy to reduce cognitive dissonance is adjusting one’s attitudes or beliefs to restore cognitive consistency, which is reflected in Lila potentially revising her stance on the harm of single-use plastic. Option B is incorrect because confirmation bias refers to the tendency to seek, interpret, or prioritize information that confirms preexisting beliefs, not the discomfort from conflicting cognitions and behaviors. Option C is incorrect because operant conditioning is a learning framework where behavior is modified through reinforcement and punishment, which is unrelated to the mental discomfort described in the scenario. Option D is incorrect because self-serving bias is the tendency to attribute personal successes to internal factors and personal failures to external factors to protect self-esteem, which does not explain Lila’s discomfort from conflicting values and actions."
}

{
  "Question": "A state appellate court in the U.S., a common law jurisdiction, is hearing a breach of contract case with facts and core legal issues nearly identical to a 2015 binding ruling from that state’s supreme court, the highest court in the jurisdiction. Under the principle of stare decisis, what is the appellate court’s required default approach to deciding this case?",
  "Options": {
    "A": "Rule based solely on the appellate court judges’ independent interpretation of state contract statutes, as the 2015 supreme court ruling is only persuasive, not binding, on lower courts",
