  },
  "CorrectAnswer": "B",
  "Explanation": "The principle of stare decisis (meaning 'to stand by things decided') is the foundational common law rule requiring courts to adhere to binding precedent from higher courts in the same jurisdiction for cases with substantially similar facts and legal issues. Departure from binding precedent is only permitted under extraordinary circumstances, such as a showing the prior ruling was clearly erroneous or would produce manifest injustice. Option A incorrectly characterizes binding higher court precedent as merely persuasive. Option C misstates procedural rules, as lower appellate courts are responsible for applying existing supreme court precedent without first remanding for clarification as a default step. Option D describes the opposite of stare decisis, which mandates deference to valid binding precedent rather than independent de novo review with no weight to prior rulings."
}

